A||N||6||Linx||stalls when hot||||1||
Z||000000||Linx||05-14-2000||02:53 PM||||Ive just rebuilt my SE's 2.8 V6.  Started on first pull!  Idles wonderfully.  Starts easily.  But when it gets hot, it stalls when I pop it into "drive" and is sometimes hard to restart.  It did this before the rebuild, but i was hopin' it'd go away.<P>IAS & TPS is good.  Yup- replaced that solenoid in the tranny (and it <I>was</I> blown, btw).  Timing dead on.  No vac' leaks.  Car uses incredible amount of gas, too.<P>I've searched old threads but didnt really find anything relevant.  Strange, too, because Ive seen this question asked before.  Looks like this is something the Fiero is prone to do, and yet no one's found a fix for it.<P>I'm thinking temp sensor- anyone got a note on what it's resistance is supposed to be when cold?  Any ideas? Anyone?  Anyone?<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 05-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||05-14-2000||02:59 PM||||How's your O2? Could be running it rich and the engine is flooded on hot restart.<P>Also, leaking injectors flood the intake and make them hard to restart. You can check that with an AC pressure gauge. Connect it to the fuel rail fitting. It shouldn't leak down anything great than 5 PSI is a couple of hours. Even that is high with good injectors.<P>Do you get any black smoke on restart?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||05-14-2000||02:59 PM||||Did you also replace the coil and rebuild the distributer? Definitely sounds like an electical problem when ever heat is involved. Start with the simple stuff first before you dig into teh temp sensors.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||05-14-2000||03:50 PM||||If it's using an "incredible" amount of gas, I would agree with the O2 sensor diagnosis...  Has it ever been replaced?  If not, that's probably your best bet.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Linx||05-15-2000||10:58 PM||||No- no black smoke. never ever.  exhaust is clear.<P>I dont think it's the O2 sensor...Ive measured it's output at around 130 millivolts.  I'm not sure what it's <I>supposed</I> to be, but it's output is stable.  Kooky thing...I disconnected the O2 sensor...and never got an ECM code.<P>I did, however, remove the IAS and retract it's plunger completely.  Reinstalled. And my car idles better and doesnt stall when shifted into "drive".  Hmmm.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||05-16-2000||01:24 PM||||Linx,<P>Here's a link for sensor resistance values. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html</A> <P>My guess: The coolant sensor is inaccurately cold (or the car is really running cold) and the ECM is not switching into closed loop (O2 sensor) mode. That would explain why you don't get a code with the O2 sensor unplugged since it is ignored when engine is cold anyway. O2 sensor voltage should vary when in closed loop mode... the steady voltage is another indication you are staying in open loop mode. If the ECM thinks the engine is at say -20 degrees that would make it very hard to start when warm.<P>Dumb question... Does the Sevice Engine light work when ignition is on and car not running?<P>Good luck, David.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||05-16-2000||07:10 PM||||From your description your O2 sounds DEAD. 130mV is lean and the ECM will add fuel to bring the mixture up. A stable O2 is usually bad although I've seen some 2.8L TBI in S10 that looked stable. But not on a Fiero. They will swing.<P>Sounds like you might have found your problem.<P>At anything other than WOT, the O2 should be swinging between about 100mv and 900mv. The goal is to swing evenly across 456mV.<P>TK||||reg||1
